    given-names: "William Eduardo"
    email: "wbendinelli@gmail.com"
    affiliation: "Universidade de São Paulo"
# No `doi:` and no `identifiers:` entry for this software: the Zenodo deposit
# has not been made, and a placeholder DOI in machine-readable citation metadata
# is a dead identifier that harvesters would resolve (audit 2026-09-05, M-5).
# `datapackage.json` omits its `id` for the same reason and carries
# `pending_doi: true`. Both flip together once Zenodo mints one -- see
# ROADMAP.md, "Open items". The `doi:` under `preferred-citation` below is the
# published *article*'s DOI and is real.
url: "https://github.com/wbendinelli/airline-delays"
repository-code: "https://github.com/wbendinelli/airline-delays"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - airline delays
  - air transport
  - congestion
  - low cost carriers
  - reproducible research
  - econometrics
  - machine learning
  - Brazil
  - ANAC VRA
  - open data
abstract: >-
  Reconstructs Brazil's Voo Regular Ativo (VRA) flight-leg records (ANAC,
  2000-2013, about 13.5 million legs) into a single canonical flight table,
  replicates the tables of Bendinelli, Bettini & Oliveira (2016,
  Transportation Research Part A, doi:10.1016/j.tra.2016.01.001) from public
  data only, and trains a flight-level delay predictor evaluated by a
  rolling-origin split (2006-2013). Every column that reaches a public table
  is declared in a single registry (`src/vra/registry.py`); every number in
  the README and the reports is printed by a versioned script; every
  divergence against the original article or against a private laboratory
  benchmark is declared rather than absorbed into a redefinition. Engineered
  under SAPIANS's software-engineering standard (golden path, tier C, class
  Research -- see .sapians-repo.yml); SAPIANS is William Eduardo
  Bendinelli's research-infrastructure practice, independent of, and with
  no bearing on, the 2016 article's other co-authors or their institutions.
preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: "Airline delays, congestion internalization and non-price spillover effects of low cost carrier entry"
  authors:
